Test images for .github/probes/pe-alignment-status.ps1
=====================================================

Eight prebuilt PE executables used to ask one question of a real NT kernel:
does it load a PE whose SectionAlignment is smaller than the page size?

They come in two sets of four that differ on the MACHINE AXIS ALONE:

    i386 set    Machine 0x014c / Magic 0x010b (PE32).   On an x64 runner these
                reach the loader through WOW64.
    x86-64 set  Machine 0x8664 / Magic 0x020b (PE32+).  These reach the native
                64-bit loader; no WOW64 is involved.

Both sets were built from the same source by the same tcc build with the same
flags, so a difference between them is attributable to the machine type.

They are committed as binaries deliberately.  They are NOT generated at probe
time: a generated image would measure this repo's generator, and the point of
the probe is to measure NT.

Each set carries its own positive control (default.exe / default-x64.exe) and
the probe runs each control FIRST within its half.  The controls are not
interchangeable: a 32-bit control passing shows only that the harness reaches
the WOW64 loader, which says nothing about whether it reaches the 64-bit one.


Evidence status -- read this before quoting anything below
----------------------------------------------------------
This file separates three different grades of claim.  Do not flatten them.

  [VERIFIED HERE]   Checked on the machine that assembled this directory, by
                    two independent parsers that agree.
  [SUPPLIED]        Stated by the author of the images.  Carries a name, not
                    evidence.  Reproducible by someone with the toolchain; it
                    was not reproducible here, for the reason given below.
  [MEASURED ON CI]  An observation from a named CI run against real NT.

The sha256 sums are the identity of these files.  Everything else about their
origin is provenance; if a sum ever disagrees, the provenance is void.


Source
------
hi.c (committed alongside, 51 bytes):

    #include <windows.h>
    int main(void) { return 42; }

sha256 of hi.c: 2f61c6ada042773cdd7ac63a41a3c3c5cdbba5346300c9b08bb0d1638fd7c0da
    [VERIFIED HERE]


Producing toolchain  [SUPPLIED]
-------------------------------
tcc (the Tiny C Compiler), build ID 19d68402, reporting itself as:

    tcc version 0.9.28rc 2026-08-25 mob@19d68402

Repository: github.com/Pandapip1/tinycc

IDENTICAL for both the i386 and the x86-64 set.  That is the reason the two
sets are comparable at all: same compiler build, same source, same flags, one
axis changed.

    i386 set:    T=.../i386-win32-tcc
    x86-64 set:  T=.../x86_64-win32-tcc
    both:        P=.../install/lib/tcc/win32

    invoked as:  $T -B$P -I$P/include -L$P/lib hi.c -o <out>   plus per-cell:

        default     / default-x64      (no extra flags)
        subpage20   / subpage20-x64    -Wl,--section-alignment=20 -Wl,--file-alignment=20
        subpage200  / subpage200-x64   -Wl,--section-alignment=200 -Wl,--file-alignment=200
        native20    / native20-x64     -Wl,--section-alignment=20 -Wl,--file-alignment=20
                                        -Wl,-subsystem=native

THE ALIGNMENT VALUES ABOVE ARE PARSED AS HEXADECIMAL, NOT DECIMAL.  `=20' means
0x20 and `=200' means 0x200.  Anyone "correcting" them to decimal builds
DIFFERENT IMAGES and the sha256 sums below will not reproduce.

    [VERIFIED HERE] -- read from the toolchain's own source at the stated build
    ID, not taken on trust.  In libtcc.c at commit 19d68402:

        1497:  } else if (link_option(&o, "section-alignment=")) {
        1498:      s->section_align = strtoul(o.arg, &end, 16);
        ...
        1518:  } else if (link_option(&o, "file-alignment=")) {
        1519:      s->pe_file_align = strtoul(o.arg, &end, 16);

    Base 16 in both cases.  The trap is real rather than theoretical because
    the neighbouring option is NOT hex -- `--stack=' three lines further down
    uses strtoul(..., 10) -- so there is no consistent rule to fall back on and
    the flags must be read literally.

Why this section is [SUPPLIED] and not [VERIFIED HERE]
------------------------------------------------------
The images could not be rebuilt on the machine that assembled this directory.
Its tcc is 0.9.27, x86_64 Linux, with no win32 headers and no win32 crt, so it
cannot emit a PE at all -- for either machine type.  The gap is ENVIRONMENTAL,
not an oversight: nothing here contradicts the attribution, it simply could not
be exercised.  A reader with an x86_64-win32-tcc and an i386-win32-tcc at build
19d68402 can close it by rebuilding and comparing sums.

Caveat for anyone who does reproduce this  [SUPPLIED]
-----------------------------------------------------
Rebuilding with a CURRENT tcc is expected to yield BYTE-IDENTICAL images but a
DIFFERENT stderr warning, because build 19d68402 still carries the older
warning text.  A differing warning line is therefore not evidence that the
images differ -- compare the sha256 sums, not the compiler's chatter.

Related, and worth stating plainly: at 19d68402 the sub-page warning tcc emits
reads "section alignment 0x%x is below the page size; modern Windows will not
load this image" (tccpe.c:890, and gated at :887 on the user having asked for
the alignment).  THAT STRING IS THE TOOLCHAIN'S BELIEF ABOUT NT, NOT A
MEASUREMENT OF NT.  It must not be read as a prior for any cell in this probe;
measuring that belief is the entire reason the probe exists.


