These sums are the identity of these files.  The probe recomputes them at run
time on the runner and prints measured against expected, so a mangled checkout
shows up as a hash mismatch rather than as a mysterious NTSTATUS.
.gitattributes here marks *.exe binary so no eol conversion can touch them.


Headers, read from the bytes  [VERIFIED HERE]
---------------------------------------------
Parsed twice, independently, and the two agree field for field: once by
python3 struct, and once by the probe's own ShowHeaders function, extracted
verbatim from pe-alignment-status.ps1 by the PowerShell AST parser and run
under Linux pwsh against these committed files.  Cross-checking the probe's
parser against a second implementation is the part of the probe that IS
testable off-Windows, so it was tested.

    file                 size  Machine  Magic   nsec  SzOptHdr  SectAlign  FileAlign  SizeOfImage  Subsys
    default.exe          2048  0x014c   0x010b     3     0xe0     0x1000     0x0200      0x4000      3
    subpage200.exe       2048  0x014c   0x010b     3     0xe0     0x0200     0x0200      0x0a00      3
    subpage20.exe        1568  0x014c   0x010b     3     0xe0     0x0020     0x0020      0x0460      3
    native20.exe         1568  0x014c   0x010b     3     0xe0     0x0020     0x0020      0x0460      1
    default-x64.exe      3072  0x8664   0x020b     4     0xf0     0x1000     0x0200      0x5000      3
    subpage200-x64.exe   3072  0x8664   0x020b     4     0xf0     0x0200     0x0200      0x0c00      3
    subpage20-x64.exe    2112  0x8664   0x020b     4     0xf0     0x0020     0x0020      0x0640      3
    native20-x64.exe     2112  0x8664   0x020b     4     0xf0     0x0020     0x0020      0x0640      1

    All eight: e_magic=0x5a4d, e_lfanew=0x80, "PE\0\0", TimeDateStamp=0,
    imports from kernel32.dll and msvcrt.dll.
    i386 set:   3 sections (.text/.rdata/.bss),        Characteristics 0x030f.
    x86-64 set: 4 sections (.text/.rdata/.bss/.pdata), Characteristics 0x022f.
    The extra .pdata and the larger optional header are what PE32+ requires;
    they are not an alignment difference.

The probe re-parses all of this on the runner and additionally cross-checks the
Machine and Magic it reads against the values recorded in this table.  A
disagreement is printed as a HEADER DISAGREEMENT finding about the committed
file; it is not silently reconciled.


Result on the i386 set  [MEASURED ON CI]
-----------------------------------------
Windows Server 2025 runner, GitHub Actions run 32926175575.  This is recorded
so the artifact carries its own result.

    default.exe     0x1000/0x200  CreateProcessW=True   SEC_IMAGE=0x00000000   (control: PASS)
    subpage200.exe  0x200/0x200   CreateProcessW=False err=193  SEC_IMAGE=0xC000007B
    subpage20.exe   0x20/0x20     CreateProcessW=False err=193  SEC_IMAGE=0xC000007B
    native20.exe    0x20/0x20 s1  CreateProcessW=False err=193  SEC_IMAGE=0xC000007B

    0xC000007B is STATUS_INVALID_IMAGE_FORMAT; 193 is ERROR_BAD_EXE_FORMAT.

    SEC_COMMIT returned 0x00000000 on every rejected image.  That is the
    control on the axis: the file object backs a section perfectly well, so
    what was rejected is specifically the IMAGE FORMAT, not the file.

    The positive control passed, so the harness was working and these are
    measurements of the kernel rather than of the probe.

    Scope of this result, stated exactly: on this kernel, sub-page
    SectionAlignment PE32 images were rejected via the WOW64 path -- INCLUDING
    the 0x200/0x200 case.  Every image in that set is Machine 0x014c, so this
    is an answer about the WOW64 loader and about nothing else.  The x86-64 set
    exists precisely because this result does not generalise across the machine
    axis on its own.

    NO RESULT IS RECORDED OR PREDICTED HERE FOR THE x86-64 SET.  It has not
    been run.  Do not write an expected NTSTATUS for those cells into this file
    or into the probe: the whole value of the x64 half is that its outcome is
    not assumed in advance, and an expectation recorded here would contaminate
    the reading of whatever comes back.


Roles
-----
default.exe        i386 positive control.  Ordinary 0x1000/0x200 alignment.  If
                   this does not map as SEC_IMAGE and launch, the i386 half is
                   broken and every i386 cell is void.
subpage200.exe     SectionAlignment == FileAlignment == 0x200.  The cell the
                   probe exists for.
subpage20.exe      SectionAlignment == FileAlignment == 0x20.
native20.exe       Subsystem 1 (native).  Only meaningful on the
                   NtCreateSection(SEC_IMAGE) axis; CreateProcessW does not
                   create user-mode processes from native-subsystem images, so
                   its CreateProcessW result says nothing about alignment.

default-x64.exe    x86-64 positive control, run first among the x64 cells.  If
                   this does not map as SEC_IMAGE and launch, the harness never
                   reached the native 64-bit loader and every x64 cell is void.
                   The i386 control does NOT stand in for this one.
subpage200-x64.exe SectionAlignment == FileAlignment == 0x200, PE32+.
subpage20-x64.exe  SectionAlignment == FileAlignment == 0x20, PE32+.
native20-x64.exe   Subsystem 1 (native), PE32+.  Same caveat as native20.exe:
                   only the SEC_IMAGE line is on-axis.
